```
#include<stdio.h>
int main (void)
{
  int x, *p;
  x=30;
  p=x;
  print f ("%d", *p);
  return 0;
}
```

solution:
Here p=x; is given.
In this line, p is a pointer to an integer (int *), and
x is an integer. However, the assignment p = x;
attempts to assign an integer (x) to a pointer (p).
This will result in a compilation error because we
cannot assign an integer directly to a pointer.
Corrected program should be as follows-
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void)
{
    int x, *p;
    x = 30;
    p = &x;
    printf("%d", *p);
    return 0;
}
